# MLU-Explain Article Generator

Given a machine learning or statistics topic, build an interactive visual essay
in the style of Amazon's MLU-Explain (https://mlu-explain.github.io/).
`reference/aws-mlu-explain/` is their repo, cloned as a pattern reference and a
scaffold source — never a source of prose to copy.

## Load these when you need them

This file is in context for every session, so it stays short. The detail lives
in files you read at the point you need them:

| Read | When |
|---|---|
| `reference/house-idioms.md` | before writing or editing any component or chart |
| `reference/verifying-an-article.md` | before writing the checks, and before calling an article done |
| `reference/hybrid-workflow.md` | only when asked for the cheap or hybrid workflow — delegating bulk passes to a cheaper model |

Both are lists of rules that were real bugs in articles that looked finished.
Read the relevant one rather than opening a 15KB component to relearn a
convention.

## Articles so far

`isolation-forest`, `xgboost`, `shapley-values`, `k-means`, `lightgbm`,
`autoencoders`, `smote`, `f1-score`. All eight are listed in `site/articles.json`
and published.

**`articles/smote/` is the current reference article.** It is the one to copy
from: delicensed, both check files, `verify/ship.sh`, and a `scripts/precompute.mjs`.

## The reference repo has two stacks — do not mix them

**Svelte 3 + Rollup 2 — ours.** `linear-regression`, `logistic-regression`,
`roc-auc`, `precision-recall`, `cross-validation`, `neural-networks`,
`reinforcement-learning`, `equality-of-odds`, `starter-mlu-explain`.

**Parcel + vanilla D3 + sass — a different architecture, not an analog.**
`bias-variance`, `decision-tree`, `random-forest`, `train-test-validation`,
`double-descent`, `double-descent2`, `parcel-mlu-explain-starter`.

The README omits `cross-validation`, `neural-networks`, `reinforcement-learning`
and `equality-of-odds`; they are real.

## Scaffold by selective copy — never `cp -r` the starter

`device_bash` cannot delete files on this mount and the permission request has
been refused before, so a recipe that ends in `rm` is a recipe that gets stuck.
Copy only what you want and there is nothing to delete. Copy from the newest
finished article, not from the starter: it is already delicensed and already
carries every fix.

```bash
SRC=articles/smote
DST=articles/<slug>
mkdir -p $DST/src/Components $DST/public/assets $DST/scripts $DST/verify
cp $SRC/rollup.config.js $SRC/svelte.config.js $SRC/.gitignore $DST/
cp $SRC/src/{main.js,katexify.js,rng.js,plot.js} $DST/src/
cp $SRC/src/Components/{Scrolly.svelte,Logo.svelte} $DST/src/Components/
cp $SRC/public/index.html $DST/public/
cp -r $SRC/public/assets/{fonts,katex-fonts,styles} $DST/public/assets/
cp $SRC/verify/{check-browser.mjs,ship.sh} $DST/verify/
# and src/contour.js if the article draws regions rather than only points
```

Amazon Ember, `mlu_robot.png`, the Svelte template favicon, `Scatter.js`,
`StaticChart.svelte` and `CenterScroll.svelte` never arrive, so the licensing
rules below hold by construction. Write `package.json`, `Meta.svelte`,
`public/index.html`'s `<title>`, `palette.js`, `datasets.js` and every component
fresh.

**Copy the infrastructure; copy nothing about the shape.** The file list above
is deliberately all plumbing — build config, the seeded RNG, the plot
transform, the scroll observer, the site mark, the stylesheets. `App.svelte`,
the section order and the choice of figures are decisions this topic makes for
itself, and taking them from the last article is how seven articles ended up
looking the same. See the next section.

## Structure: fixed furniture, and a shape you choose

Only the ends are fixed:

```
Meta → Logo → Title → Intro          ...the article...          Conclusion → Resources
```

What goes between them is a decision, and it is the first thing that should
differ between two articles. **All seven so far have the same shape** — a big
lab, then maths, then a scrollytelling section, then a couple of figures — and
that is convergent evolution from copying the previous article, not seven
topics that each wanted it. MLU-Explain's own articles vary far more.

Pick the shape by asking: **what does the reader have to be holding in their
head already for the interactive to mean anything?** If the honest answer is
"nothing", put it first. If it is "three ideas", earn them first.

- **Lab first.** One manipulable object carries the whole mechanism, and the
  article is an examination of it. Works when the object is legible cold.
  (`smote`, `autoencoders`)
- **Build-up.** Three or four small figures, each making one point, and the
  interactive arrives only once the reader can use it. The right shape when a
  lab shown cold would just be a picture the reader has no reason to touch.
- **One steered simulation.** The article *is* a single running thing the
  reader advances, prose alongside it. Natural for anything iterative — an
  optimiser, a clustering loop, boosting rounds.
- **Comparison spine.** Two things side by side the whole way down; the article
  is the accumulating differences. For genuinely comparative topics, this beats
  bolting a second panel onto a lab.
- **Question first.** Open on a concrete puzzle the reader can attempt, and let
  the machinery arrive as the answer. `shapley-values` is naturally this shape.

These combine, and the list is not exhaustive. State the chosen shape in pass 1
alongside the hook, and say why the topic wants it — "the default" is not a
reason.

Component conventions, whatever the shape: name the hook for the topic
(`ProjectionLab`, `SmoteLab`), never `LineChart`. Avoid the starter's
centre-scroll pattern — a text card floating over a sticky chart covered the
chart in both articles that used it; side-by-side on desktop, folding to
stacked on mobile, is what reads.

## Commands

Inside an article directory:

- `npm install` — once
- `npm run dev` — the preview loop, http://localhost:5000, livereload
- `npm run build` — production build into `public/build/`
- `npm run precompute` — regenerates `src/precomputed.js` (only if the article has one)
- `npm run check` — `verify/check-numbers.mjs`
- `./verify/ship.sh` — build, refuse warnings, prove the build ran, run the
  numbers, and produce a verified tarball for the browser checks.
  `-m 'a string I just added'` proves your edit reached the bundle.

## Build a new article in four passes

Long single sessions are the main cost driver — the whole conversation is
re-sent with every tool call. Clear between passes and write the handoff down.

1. **Design.** Pick the angle (prefer narrow and deep over survey). Then, before
   any component exists:
   - **Write down the standard telling as a list of claims** — what a textbook,
     a docs page or a good blog post says this technique does and why. Four or
     five sentences, stated flatly enough to be wrong.
   - Write `datasets.js` and the topic module, and run throwaway probe scripts
     in node (`node --input-type=module`) that test *those specific claims*.
   - The gap is the article. A textbook explanation is a compression, and the
     part worth writing is where the compression is lossy: which claim is only
     true in a regime nobody mentions, which one is exactly true and for a
     different reason than given, which one is a memorised phrase. Every
     article's best paragraph so far came from this and none of them were
     looking for it.
   - Record the list and the verdicts in the article's `README.md`, and assert
     each verdict in `verify/check-numbers.mjs`.
   Write a one-page spec to the project. Needs the strongest model.
2. **Build.** Scaffold and write every component from that spec, with
   `reference/house-idioms.md` open. Sonnet handles this well.
3. **Verify.** `./verify/ship.sh`, then the browser checks, then fix what they
   find. Mostly mechanical — Sonnet or Haiku.
4. **Review.** Read the prose against the charts, run the final read-through in
   `reference/verifying-an-article.md`, fix the claims. Needs the strongest
   model; it is where the judgement is.

At the start of pass 1, state three things: the slug, the single interactive
hook, and the closest structural analog.

## Non-negotiables

- **Write every word from scratch.** Never copy prose from the reference repo.
- **No Amazon Ember.** `public/assets/mlu-fonts/` is licensed for local viewing
  only — the header in `font.css` says so. The articles ship self-hosted Inter
  and IBM Plex Mono. `build-site.sh` and `ship.sh` both refuse an article that
  still has it.
- **No MLU-Explain branding in the page furniture.** Their robot and wordmark in
  the masthead make the page read as an MLU-Explain publication. Use the site
  mark in `articles/smote/src/Components/Logo.svelte`. Attribution belongs in
  the conclusion and the references, which is what CC BY-SA actually asks for.
- **Nothing ships unverified.** Both check files pass, and somebody has looked
  at the screenshots.

## Editorial voice

Short visual essays, not textbook chapters.

- Second person, conversational. "Drag the point below…"
- **One primary hook, and small interactions everywhere else.** The primary
  hook is a single manipulable object carrying the central claim, and it stays
  simple: if it needs ten controls, split it and hang the secondary parameters
  on a figure attached to the prose that explains them. That was the XGBoost
  article's real problem.
- But one interactive is a **floor, not a ceiling**, and reading it as a
  ceiling is what produces an article with a lab at the top and nothing to
  touch for the next four screens. Anywhere a sentence would be better as a
  control, make it one: a toggle inside a paragraph, a scrubber on a figure, a
  preset button that jumps a chart to the case the paragraph is describing. A
  reader who stops touching things has usually stopped reading.
- Small interactions are held to the same standard as the hook — reactive
  helpers, a clamped measured width, a `viewBox`, and something in
  `check-browser.mjs` that exercises them. An interaction nothing checks will
  quietly break.
- Every article has to do five things: motivate the problem, show the
  mechanism, let the reader work it, give the maths, and say what it costs you.
  Their *order* comes from the shape you chose, not from this list. Written in
  the order above they read as the default shape, which is the point of
  choosing one.
- The chart carries the explanation; the prose says what to look at.
- Be fair to whatever the article argues against, and give it its best case.

## Publishing

`site/` is a separate git repository holding the built static site — the
generator, `reference/` (243MB) and every `node_modules` stay local.

```bash
./scripts/build-site.sh
```

It builds each article listed in `site/articles.json`, copies its `public/` into
`site/<slug>/` without source maps, and regenerates `site/index.html` from the
manifest. **An article goes live only when it has an entry in
`site/articles.json`**, so unfinished ones sit in `articles/` untouched.

Deployed by GitHub Pages from the `site/` repo's `main` at the repository root.
`.nojekyll` is committed. Every asset path is relative, so the site works at any
base path without changes.
